    "created_at": "2016-11-23T23:03:22Z",
    "updated_at": "2016-11-23T23:03:22Z",
    "body": "Yep, this is a longstanding issue.  One problem is that promise microtasks are not really associated with a particular global.  So nothing in current specs really prevents them from continuing to execute, not just when a browsing context is destroyed but also when the \"page involved\" (whatever that means) is navigated away from _without_ destroying the browsing context.\r\n\r\nChrome does in fact abort scheduled microtasks in some browsing context destruction cases but not others.  For example, closing a tab in Chrome might abort them (maybe _does_ abort them?  I haven't tested extensively).\r\n\r\nI think from a user point of view it's super-surprising if closing a tab does not abort some sort of promise resolution loop happening in that tab.  The question is how to spec this reasonably in the various edge cases (promise resolution loop spanning multiple globals, etc, etc).\r\n\r\nThis also matters in terms of things like fetch() that return promises.  Is that promise rejected when the fetch() is aborted due to unload?  Due to browser context destruction?  If so, is the rejection observable in various circumstances?  I believe this case is what prompted Ben's questions.\r\n\r\nIt would be good to have something specified here that doesn't depend on implementation details like \"closing a tab can kill that OS process\", not least because it would be nice if tab closing behavior were the same no matter whether other unrelated tabs happen to be in that process.  ;)",
    "author_association": "CONTRIBUTOR",
